{"kind":"request","text":"hugh: play the chess app as a first-time user and say whether it is actually fixed. The whole lane is waiting on your answer and, until now, nobody had actually asked you — which is my omission.\n\nWHY THIS COMES TO YOU. The lane exists because of your usability review of the landed app. Codex and I have taken it through five heads and four verdicts, and the review gate is now clear: exact head b9483eaad39d27bc2747f5e8ec0c1501d03e4e2f is approved at cba97723 and ratified, with all ten exact-path artifacts co-signed. Codex proposed, and I agreed, that the merge should still wait for your acceptance rather than ours. That was the right instinct: we can verify that the code does what we told each other it should, and we cannot verify that a first-time user has a good time. Only you can say that.\n\nBut the agreement lived in an assert and in chat between codex and me. There was no row on your board, so the lane was parked on a person who had never been given anything to answer. That is the failure the durable record exists to prevent and I should have filed this when the agreement was made.\n\nWHAT WE CHANGED, so you know what to look for rather than hunting. The refusal reason for an illegal move now tells the truth: a bare promotion like a7b8 used to be refused with \"destination b8 is not valid from a7\", which was false and pointed you away from the move that works; it now names the missing promotion piece and gives you examples, and the README teaches the fifth character. Named games are created in one atomic act, so a failed creation can no longer leave a real, joinable game in the log whose identifier you were never shown. Restricted games no longer describe themselves as open: an empty seat on a secret or invite-restricted game says so on both the lobby card and the game page, and the \"ask your agent to join\" instruction appears only where a join would actually succeed.\n\nWHAT I AM ASKING. Start from the README as a new user would, create a game, play some moves against a second key, try a promotion, and look at the lobby and a game page for an open and a restricted game. Then say whether the first-user experience is fixed in your judgement. A plain yes is enough to release the merge. If it is not fixed, say what is still wrong in whatever form suits you and I will turn it into the next lane.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASKING. Not a code review — that is done, twice over, with the evidence in the verdicts. Not a gate run. Not a decision about the durable record. Just the thing only you can supply: whether a person meeting this for the first time gets on with it.\n\nThe branch is request/chess-first-user at that exact head, unchanged and clean. Nobody will merge it until you answer, and if you would rather it merged now and be judged in main, say that instead and I will lift the hold.","body":{"branch":"request/chess-first-user","conditions":"Hugh exercises the chess app as a first-time user at exact head b9483eaad39d27bc2747f5e8ec0c1501d03e4e2f and records an acceptance or a refusal of the first-user experience. An acceptance releases the merge hold that codex and planner agreed to; a refusal names what remains wrong, in any form, and planner turns it into the next lane. Alternatively hugh may direct that the head merge now and be judged in main, which also satisfies this request. No code review, gate run, or durable-record decision is asked for.","head":"b9483eaad39d27bc2747f5e8ec0c1501d03e4e2f","severity":"normal","to":"63a5ef314054288050ed4b10a49f1bd0ccae028d51eed93d5b93a51b101d8a02"}}